          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Why is it hard to keep taking a preventer inhaler every day when you feel fine?",
          "answer": "A daily preventer inhaler works by calming airway inflammation over time, so you usually can't feel it working. A reliever inhaler gives quick relief you can feel, so people often judge the two by how they feel that day. The asthma adherence guidance notes that the daily inhaler is often the first one dropped when someone feels well, which is when it is working best (*Sticking with asthma treatment: inhaler technique, controllers and action plans*).\n\nCommon reasons people stop or skip doses include:\n\n- **Not noticing a benefit.** Nothing feels different, so the habit seems pointless.\n- **Poor inhaler technique.** If the technique is wrong, less medicine reaches the lungs, and the person may feel the treatment isn't helping. The same source says technique is often the real problem and is commonly not checked.\n- **Confusing the two devices.** Someone may not be sure which inhaler is the daily one and which is for quick relief.\n- **Routine and forgetting.** The guidance suggests tying doses to an existing daily habit rather than a clock time, and using reminders.\n\nSome practical steps the guidance describes:\n\n- Ask a clinician or pharmacist to check your inhaler technique, and ask them to recheck it at reviews.\n- Keep a written action plan that says what to take every day, what to do when symptoms get worse, and when to seek help. Keep a copy on your phone and share it with family.\n- Tell your clinician if you are using the reliever inhaler more often. The asthma guidance says rising reliever use usually means the condition is not well controlled, not that the reliever is doing its job.\n- Raise cost or side-effect worries with your clinician. These are common reasons for missed doses and are often not mentioned unless asked about.\n\nFor COPD, the guidance I retrieved covers review after every flare-up and technique checks at each review, but I could not retrieve the COPD-specific adherence guidance. For advice on your own treatment, please speak with your clinician or pharmacist.\n\nIf you have sudden trouble breathing, difficulty speaking in full sentences, blue lips or fingers, or confusion, call emergency services straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
